WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.685
Two questions people mix up.

2
00:00:01.965 --> 00:00:03.629
“Which coding agent is best?

3
00:00:03.909 --> 00:00:05.595
” hides two different questions.

4
00:00:05.875 --> 00:00:13.640
One is about capability: on a fixed set of tasks with an automatic check, how often does an agent produce a change that passes?

5
00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:15.349
Benchmarks answer that.

6
00:00:15.629 --> 00:00:23.501
The other is about impact: when real developers use these tools on their real work, do they ship more, faster, at the same quality?

7
00:00:23.781 --> 00:00:27.493
Only studies of people answer that, and they are much harder to run.

8
00:00:27.773 --> 00:00:29.480
This lesson takes each in turn.

9
00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:40.960
You have met SWE-bench before, in Studios 1 and 3; here the focus is what changed in 2025 and 2026 and how to read the newer benchmarks.

10
00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:44.909
Then comes the productivity evidence, which is genuinely mixed.

11
00:00:45.189 --> 00:00:46.747
The goal is not a verdict.

12
00:00:47.027 --> 00:00:53.896
It’s to leave you able to read a launch post, a leaderboard, or a vendor case study and say precisely what it shows.
